===Etymology 1===
{{root|en|ine-pro|*gʰerdʰ-}}
From {{inh|en|enm|girden}}, {{m|enm|gerden}}, {{m|enm|gurden|gürden}}, from {{inh|en|ang|gyrdan||to put a belt around, to put a girdle around}}, from {{inh|en|gem-pro|*gurdijaną|t=to gird}}, from {{der|en|ine-pro|*gʰerdʰ-}}. Cognate with {{cog|fy|gurdzje}}, {{m|fy|girdzje}}, {{cog|nl|gorden}}, {{cog|de|gürten}}, {{cog|sv|gjorda}}, {{cog|is|gyrða}}, {{cog|sq|ngërthej||to tie together by weaving, to bind}}.

====Verb====
{{en-verb|past2=girt}}

# {{lb|en|transitive}} To [[bind]] with a [[flexible]] [[rope]] or [[cord]].
#: ''The fasces were '''girt''' about with twine in bundles large.''
# {{lb|en|transitive}} To [[encircle]] with, or as if with a [[belt]].
#: ''The lady '''girt''' herself with silver chain, from which she hung a golden shear.''
#: ''Our home is '''girt''' by sea...'' - [[w:Advance Australia Fair|Advance Australia Fair]]
#* {{quote-book|1=en|year=1887|author=H. Rider Haggard|authorlink=H. Rider Haggard|title=She: A History of Adventure|url=http://www.gutenberg.org/files/3155/3155-0.txt|passage=It took me back across the dim gulf of ages to some happy home in dead Imperial K&ocirc;r, where this winsome lady '''girt''' about with beauty had lived and died, and dying taken her last-born with her to the tomb.}}
# {{lb|en|transitive|reflexive}} To [[prepare]] oneself for an [[action]].

